model {
  extend basalt.modelGateway {
    metadata {
      constraint "Percentage notifications require a breached one-hour or twenty-four-hour window with at least one hundred real requests in that same window and a real failure in the latest five-minute window. Notification eligibility never rewrites the raw success rate, counts or objective state. The pool app displays the raw one-hour and twenty-four-hour real success rates and counts independently of recent incidents, and annotates nonempty samples below one hundred real requests. Its existing optional reliability read batches both windows in one statement and preserves the usage snapshot on failure. Proof traffic cannot meet the volume or recency conditions. Immediate real-failure, synthetic availability and collection-integrity signals keep their independent behavior."
      constraint "The overlapping five-minute, one-hour and twenty-four-hour SLO windows aggregate one materialized request selection in the caller's existing snapshot. Raw request times retain nanosecond text boundaries; terminal finishes retain their timestamp boundaries. Global latest-attempt selection, terminal-only fallback, proof classification and unavailable-collection rules remain identical in each window."
      constraint "Request reliability counts only the retained local model and gateway sources. Explicit purpose records match the exact pool, source and hashed client key; unclassified keys count as real. Proof counts remain visible separately, while token and cost totals retain every request. The same SQL read supplies the overview and rolling five-minute, one-hour and twenty-four-hour request windows. Empty real traffic has no success rate. Optional reliability reads use a savepoint so an unavailable monitor cannot discard the usage, token or cost answers. Availability RPC failure retains an independently measured reliability verdict and both closed failure classes. An inactive, stale, lagging or unclassified capture cannot establish the objective. The once-per-minute availability check-in reports only the synthetic pool probe. Real-request success below 99 percent emits its own error through the existing observability route. Missing collection remains unknown in the interface and emits a separate reliability incident with bounded counts and a closed reason. It never becomes an availability outage. Recent real failures emit their own signal even when the longer success objective is met. Empty real traffic and proof-only failures do not report an outage. Before dispatch, the Worker assigns a request identity and records metadata-only start and terminal evidence. Home usage remains append only. Video job status remains separate from successful HTTP receipt or poll delivery. Terminal failure overlays the final matching Home attempt; finished requests awaiting Home collection count once and disappear from that fallback when usage arrives. Missing terminal tables or abandoned starts cannot establish a green objective. Counts and closed reasons leave the service; keys and request content do not. Terminal observation selects ArrayBuffer delivery on both accepted WebSocket peers before forwarding or inspecting binary frames. Runtime Blob defaults must not change payload bytes, hide request starts or discard private consumer receipts. Response adapters preserve the private model lifetime binding, including selected and legacy Responses sockets. Terminal collection readiness and the correlation cutover use the named v7 marker independently of later import completion migrations."
      constraint "Release provisioning applies the additive named traffic-purpose schema through the shared model-history migrator before Worker activation. Migration failure blocks activation; the private reviewed schema operation uses the same runner. It never rewrites request history. Purpose changes use the private operations entrypoint with a dry run listing exact retained request identifiers, current classifications and an evidence hash. Applying requires the digest of that selection; a changed selection refuses. A started local receipt prevents replay after an unknown apply outcome. Restoring a proof key to real uses the same reviewed path. Public app and model routes cannot change purpose."
      constraint "A gateway refusal records its history row under the attempt's request identity, which the request's terminal evidence and Home usage share. A runtime endpoint's correlation id stays only in the refusal log line and the client-visible request id. Terminal evidence counts a request only when no retained Home attempt or gateway refusal already holds a row with its identity, so a retried request refused before dispatch counts once."
    }
  }
  basalt.telemetry -[sync]-> basalt.pool "read request reliability contracts for monitor diagnostics"
}
